==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|stelan|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*stelaną}}. Compare Low German {{term|stehlen|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|stelen|lang=nl}}, English {{term|steal|lang=en}}, Danish {{term|stjæle|lang=da}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃteːlən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-stehlen.ogg|audio|lang=de}}
* {{homophones|Stelen|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=4|stiehlt|stahl|gestohlen}}

# {{context|transitive|or|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[steal]]

====Usage notes====
The one from whom something is stolen is given in the dative case. Thus, “''Er hat '''mir''' ein Buch gestohlen''” means: “He stole a book from me.” This is quite contrary to English usage, where “He stole me a book” would be the equivalent of German: “''Er hat ein Buch '''für''' mich gestohlen.''” 
(No, that is 'He stole for me, a book' or 'He stole a book for me.'). Literally, the German translated would turn out as 'He has a book for me, stolen', or; 'He has stolen a book for me', which is not really very different at all, but, in fact nearly identical. With proper english syntax, you also need to stipulate the correct prepositions; 'for' or 'from' (which is given by the declination of mich to mir in this particular example).

